// src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/sig_verify.pdx -- R52.M4-005 (#1701)
//
// PDXFS-ON-BLOCK v1 -- INODE SIG-TAIL: READ / PREFIX-TEAR DETECT /
//                       (STUBBED) BLAKE3 HASH VERIFY.
//
// Design authority: design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §2.4
// (R52.M4, step 5: "#1701 -- Sig-tail read: fetch signature block at
// inode's sig_hash referent ... BLAKE3-verify sig_hash == blake3
// (sig_block); prefix-tear detect sig_prefix == sig_block[0..24];
// ML-DSA-65 verify signature against inode-content bytes [0, 72) and
// the volume's sig_key_hash public key."); on-disk row layout frozen
// at design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.3, whose "Signed-inode
// tail -- how it fits" prose gives the two-step verification order this
// file follows: "(1) Read the inode block; check sig_prefix matches
// the prefix of the signature block the walker fetches. (2) Read the
// signature block; verify blake3(sig_bytes) == sig_hash."
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HARD BLOCKER -- no BLAKE3 kernel/.pdx primitive exists (softarch gap,
// see design plan §5.4 point 2)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §5.4 flags BLAKE3-hash-32
// (`blake3_hash32(msg_ptr, msg_len, out_ptr) -> ()`) as a soft cross-repo
// blocker: "Likely present from R41 semantic-terminal work -- softarch
// to verify at R52.M1 landing with a targeted grep. If absent, file
// paideia-as issue immediately (M1 blocks by grep, not by build)."
//
// That grep was re-run at this landing (R52.M4-005):
//
//   grep -rn 'blake3\|BLAKE3' src/kernel/          -> zero hits under
//     src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/, src/kernel/core/crypto/, or anywhere
//     else in the kernel tree. The only in-tree kernel BLAKE3
//     REFERENCES are comments/field-name mirrors in superblock.pdx /
//     superblock_fmt.pdx describing the ON-DISK sig_key_hash field's
//     PROVENANCE -- no callable primitive.
//
//   grep -rn 'blake3\|BLAKE3' tools/paideia-as/    -> extensive hits,
//     but every one is HOST-SIDE (the Rust toolchain's own PAX
//     object-format content hashing in paideia-as-linker /
//     paideia-as-emitter-pax / paideia-pq-sign, via the Rust `blake3`
//     crate). None of this is reachable from `.pdx` kernel source --
//     it hashes the COMPILER's OUTPUT ARTIFACTS, not something a
//     compiled kernel can call at runtime.
//
//   tools/paideia-as/RELEASE-NOTES-v0.20.md (self-hosting audit,
//     PA-R20-008) explicitly lists "BLAKE3 in `.pdx`" as one of five
//     Class-A self-hosting blockers -- "~500 LoC of careful work with
//     no language gaps... Deferred to v0.21 phase." I.e. paideia-as's
//     own maintainers have already scoped this as future work, not
//     something silently missing.
//
// Conclusion: blake3_hash32 is a CONFIRMED cross-repo gap, not merely
// "likely absent." Per this issue's own constraints ("If not, defer to
// a subsequent M4 iteration") and per the codebase's established
// fail-closed precedent for an unimplemented crypto primitive
// (pdxfs_lite/verify.pdx's `ml_dsa_verify_stub` occupies its symbol
// slot and is NOT wired into any live accept path), this file lands:
//
//   (a) REAL, working code for the two BLAKE3-independent pieces of
//       #1701's scope -- sig-tail read (sig_tail_read) and prefix-tear
//       detection (sig_prefix_tear_detect), both pure byte
//       copy/compare over already-resident memory, no missing
//       primitive required.
//   (b) A STUB for the BLAKE3 hash-verify piece (sig_hash_verify_stub)
//       that occupies the symbol slot the real primitive will fill
//       once paideia-as ships blake3_hash32, and is documented UNIMPL.
//       It returns INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH (0xFFFFED1C) UNCONDITIONALLY
//       -- fail-closed, matching this codebase's own secure-by-default
//       posture (see pdxfs_lite/verify.pdx header, mode (3): "Neither
//       flag set: return 0 (reject). Secure-by-default."). It never
//       reports a false PASS.
//   (c) The umbrella `sig_tail_verify` matching the plan's exact
//       5-arg signature (§5.1's arity note: "Sig-tail verify takes
//       (inode_row_ptr, sig_key_hash_ptr, sig_key_material_ptr,
//       sig_block_ptr, out_verify_result_ptr) = 5. Fits."), which
//       chains (a)+(b) in the documented order and writes the verdict
//       to *out_verify_result_ptr.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SECOND GAP (not this issue's blocker, but load-bearing for scope) --
// ML-DSA-65 verify is ALSO not yet a real accept path
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1701's plan text names a third check: "ML-DSA-65 verify signature
// against inode-content bytes [0, 72) and the volume's sig_key_hash
// public key." design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §3's R51
// dependency matrix footnotes this milestone's non-R51 gate as
// "paideia-as BLAKE3-hash-32 (verify present)" -- i.e. the plan's own
// authors believed ML-DSA-65 VERIFY was already landed (citing
// R33-crypto-kdf) and only BLAKE3 was outstanding. A grep of
// src/kernel/core/driver/sig_verify.pdx (the only in-tree ML-DSA-65
// verify surface -- driver_sig_verify / driver_sig_verify_algo, R29.M4-
// 001 #1032) shows this is only PARTIALLY true: the structural-gate
// wrapper exists and is callable, but its terminal branch is still the
// R29 scaffold -- `_sig_scaffold_epoch = 1`, and the file's own header
// states "At R29 the terminal branch cannot produce SIG_OK... R32
// replaces the SIG_UNVERIFIABLE arm with a call into ml_dsa_verify."
// No `ml_dsa_verify` (the real lattice primitive) exists anywhere in
// src/kernel/ at this landing (confirmed by grep) -- R32 has not
// landed. So even wiring driver_sig_verify into this file's step 3
// today would be dead code that can never return SIG_OK, mirroring
// exactly the BLAKE3 gap's shape.
//
// Because this file's own step 2 (BLAKE3) is fail-closed and always
// rejects, step 3 (ML-DSA-65) is UNREACHABLE regardless -- a
// fetched-and-hash-verified signature is the precondition for trusting
// it enough to run the (currently scaffold-only) lattice verify against
// it. Wiring an unreachable call to a scaffold-only primitive would add
// complexity with zero test coverage and no way to exercise the real
// path. This landing therefore reserves INODE_SIG_VERIFY_FAIL
// (0xFFFFED1D) for that future step (per inode_table.pdx's own
// "reserved for #1701" comment) but does not call driver_sig_verify
// here. Flagged for softarch / a follow-up M4 iteration once BOTH
// blake3_hash32 (paideia-as) AND ml_dsa_verify (R32, real lattice
// verifier) exist -- at that point sig_tail_verify's step 2 gains a
// real body and step 3 becomes reachable in the same commit.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scope landed
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   sig_tail_read(inode_row_ptr, out_sig_ptr) -> u64
//     Copies the inode row's 24-byte sig_prefix (offset 72) and 32-byte
//     sig_hash (offset 96) into a caller-supplied 56-byte scratch at
//     out_sig_ptr (sig_prefix at +0..24, sig_hash at +24..56). Pure
//     copy; always returns 0.
//
//   sig_prefix_tear_detect(inode_row_ptr, sig_block_ptr) -> u64
//     Compares the inode row's 24-byte sig_prefix (offset 72) against
//     the first 24 bytes of the fetched signature block at
//     sig_block_ptr. Returns 0 on match (no tear), or
//     INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR (0xFFFFED1B) on mismatch.
//
//   sig_hash_verify_stub(sig_block_ptr, sig_hash_ptr) -> u64
//     UNIMPL placeholder for `blake3(sig_block) == *sig_hash_ptr`.
//     Both arguments are accepted (unused at this landing) so the ABI
//     the real primitive will fill is already frozen and no call site
//     needs to change when blake3_hash32 lands. Always returns
//     INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH (0xFFFFED1C), fail-closed.
//
//   sig_tail_verify(inode_row_ptr, sig_key_hash_ptr,
//                   sig_key_material_ptr, sig_block_ptr,
//                   out_verify_result_ptr) -> u64
//     The umbrella entry point named by #1701's arity note. Runs
//     sig_prefix_tear_detect first (cheap, real); on a tear, writes
//     INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR to *out_verify_result_ptr and returns it
//     without touching the (currently stubbed) hash check. On a
//     prefix match, runs sig_hash_verify_stub, writes its verdict
//     (currently always INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH) to
//     *out_verify_result_ptr, and returns it. sig_key_hash_ptr /
//     sig_key_material_ptr are accepted per the frozen 5-arg ABI but
//     unused at this landing -- reserved for the step-3 ML-DSA-65 call
//     (see gap above).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOT landed here (explicitly out of scope / blocked)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   * A real blake3_hash32 call in sig_hash_verify_stub -- blocked on
//     the paideia-as gap documented above.
//   * A real ml_dsa_verify call as sig_tail_verify's step 3 -- blocked
//     on R32 (the real lattice verifier), and unreachable today
//     regardless because step 2 always fails closed.
//   * Resolving "the signature block at inode's sig_hash referent" to
//     a concrete on-disk address. Neither the 128-byte inode row (see
//     inode_table.pdx's frozen layout comment) nor volume-fs-
//     substrate.md §2.3's prose names a `sig_block_lba` field or any
//     other addressing scheme for the per-inode signature block
//     ("the full signature lives in a per-inode signature block in the
//     data region, allocated at inode-write time" -- no allocator/
//     addressing detail given). This is a genuine design-doc gap this
//     landing surfaces rather than papers over: every function below
//     takes sig_block_ptr as an ALREADY-RESOLVED in-memory pointer
//     (mirroring how inode_read/inode_write already take a resolved
//     row_ptr), leaving "how the caller obtains that pointer" to
//     whichever future issue lands the sig-block allocation/addressing
//     scheme (flagged for softarch).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Failure taxonomy -- reuses the M4 band's #1701-reserved slots (see
// inode_table.pdx header; design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md
// §4/§7.2). Restated here per this codebase's no-cross-module-constant-
// import convention (inode_table.pdx gap 4 already sets this precedent).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR = 0xFFFFED1B  wired: sig_prefix_tear_detect /
//                                         sig_tail_verify step 1.
//   INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH = 0xFFFFED1C  wired (fail-closed stub):
//                                         sig_hash_verify_stub /
//                                         sig_tail_verify step 2.
//   INODE_SIG_VERIFY_FAIL = 0xFFFFED1D  reserved, NOT wired this
//                                         landing (see gap above) --
//                                         defined here for a future
//                                         step-3 ML-DSA-65 call to
//                                         reuse without a band edit.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Arity discipline (design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §5.1)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sig_tail_read(inode_row_ptr, out_sig_ptr) = 2.
// sig_prefix_tear_detect(inode_row_ptr, sig_block_ptr) = 2.
// sig_hash_verify_stub(sig_block_ptr, sig_hash_ptr) = 2.
// sig_tail_verify(inode_row_ptr, sig_key_hash_ptr, sig_key_material_ptr,
// sig_block_ptr, out_verify_result_ptr) = 5 -- exactly the arity the
// plan's own §5.1 table names for this function ("Fits."). Every arity
// sits at or under the paideia-as ceiling of 6.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// sig_tail_read / sig_prefix_tear_detect / sig_hash_verify_stub: leaf
// functions, no nested calls, no callee-save pushes needed (every live
// value fits in caller-save rax/rcx/rdx/r10/r11 per SysV).
// sig_tail_verify: 3-push prologue (rbx=inode_row_ptr, r12=sig_block_ptr,
// r13=out_verify_result_ptr) lands rsp%16==0 for its 2 nested calls
// (entry rsp%16==8 + 24 = 32). sig_key_hash_ptr/sig_key_material_ptr
// are read directly from rsi/rdx at entry and not preserved (unused
// this landing; see gap above) -- matches pdxfs_sb_verify_sig's own
// precedent of an ABI-reserved-but-unused argument.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// mov_b / no-test / label-prefix discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No byte-granularity memory access anywhere -- every copy/compare
// below moves whole qwords (24 B = 3 qwords, 32 B = 4 qwords, 56 B = 7
// qwords), mirroring inode_table.pdx's own whole-qword-only posture.
// No test instruction anywhere; every zero/nonzero check uses
// cmp reg, imm / cmp reg, reg + je/jne. Every label is prefixed with
// its owning function's abbreviation (`str_` for sig_tail_read, `sptd_`
// for sig_prefix_tear_detect, `stv_` for sig_tail_verify;
// sig_hash_verify_stub needs none -- straight-line body), per the
// reserved-label discipline.

module SigVerify = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // M4 failure taxonomy -- #1701's reserved slice of the frozen M4 band
  // (0xFFFFED18..ED1F). inode_table.pdx ALREADY declares
  // `pub let INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR/INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH/
  // INODE_SIG_VERIFY_FAIL` (reserved there for this very issue) -- this
  // file does NOT redeclare those symbol names (that would be a
  // duplicate-definition hazard under the flat symbol namespace; see
  // allocator.pdx's own ALLOC_BITMAP_TORN, which is likewise declared
  // exactly once and reused elsewhere only as a restated literal). Every
  // reference below uses the raw hex literal with a comment naming its
  // source, exactly matching inode_table.pdx gap 4's own precedent for
  // reusing ALLOC_BITMAP_TORN (0xFFFFED11) without redeclaring it:
  //
  //   INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR   = 0xFFFFED1B  (inode_table.pdx)
  //   INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH = 0xFFFFED1C  (inode_table.pdx)
  //   INODE_SIG_VERIFY_FAIL    = 0xFFFFED1D  (inode_table.pdx; reserved,
  //                                            not wired this landing)
  // ============================================================================
  // Geometry constants -- inode row offsets frozen at inode_table.pdx /
  // design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.3.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let SIG_PREFIX_OFFSET : u64 = 72   // inode_row + 72 -> sig_prefix[24]
  pub let SIG_PREFIX_BYTES  : u64 = 24
  pub let SIG_HASH_OFFSET   : u64 = 96   // inode_row + 96 -> sig_hash[32]
  pub let SIG_HASH_BYTES    : u64 = 32

  // ============================================================================
  // sig_tail_read(inode_row_ptr: u64, out_sig_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   Copies sig_prefix (24 B, 3 qwords) from inode_row_ptr+72 into
  //   out_sig_ptr+0, then sig_hash (32 B, 4 qwords) from
  //   inode_row_ptr+96 into out_sig_ptr+24. Caller supplies a 56-byte
  //   (7-qword) scratch at out_sig_ptr. Leaf; always returns 0.
  // ============================================================================
  pub let sig_tail_read : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (inode_row_ptr: u64) (out_sig_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M4-005 (#1701). Leaf, no prologue needed (no nested calls; rax/rcx/r10/r11 all SysV caller-save, enough live registers for a 7-qword copy). Body: (1) r10=inode_row_ptr+72 (sig_prefix base); r11=out_sig_ptr. (2) copy 3 qwords r10[0..3]->r11[0..3] (sig_prefix, 24 B) via 3 unrolled mov pairs (no loop needed for a fixed tiny count, matching superblock.pdx's small fixed-field-copy style). (3) r10=inode_row_ptr+96 (sig_hash base); copy 4 qwords r10[0..4]->r11[24..28]*8-byte-stride i.e. out_sig_ptr+24..56 (sig_hash, 32 B) via 4 unrolled mov pairs. (4) xor eax,eax; ret. No test instruction; no mov_b (whole-qword copies only). Reserved labels: none needed (straight-line body, str_ prefix reserved for symmetry but unused).",
      block: {
        mov r10, rdi;
        add r10, 72;
        mov r11, rsi;

        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        mov [r11 + 0], rax;
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        mov [r11 + 8], rax;
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        mov [r11 + 16], rax;

        mov r10, rdi;
        add r10, 96;

        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        mov [r11 + 24], rax;
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        mov [r11 + 32], rax;
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        mov [r11 + 40], rax;
        mov rax, [r10 + 24];
        mov [r11 + 48], rax;

        xor eax, eax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // sig_prefix_tear_detect(inode_row_ptr: u64, sig_block_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   Compares the inode row's 24-byte sig_prefix (offset 72) against
  //   the first 24 bytes of the fetched signature block at
  //   sig_block_ptr. Returns 0 on match (no tear), or
  //   INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR (0xFFFFED1B) on the first mismatching
  //   qword. Leaf.
  // ============================================================================
  pub let sig_prefix_tear_detect : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (inode_row_ptr: u64) (sig_block_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M4-005 (#1701). Leaf, no prologue needed (no nested calls). Body: (1) r10=inode_row_ptr+72 (sig_prefix base); r11=sig_block_ptr. (2) compare 3 qwords: rax=[r10+0]; rcx=[r11+0]; cmp rax,rcx; jne sptd_tear. rax=[r10+8]; rcx=[r11+8]; cmp rax,rcx; jne sptd_tear. rax=[r10+16]; rcx=[r11+16]; cmp rax,rcx; jne sptd_tear. (3) xor eax,eax; ret (all 3 qwords equal -- no tear). sptd_tear: mov rax,0xFFFFED1B; ret. No test instruction; no mov_b. Reserved labels: sptd_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, rdi;
        add r10, 72;
        mov r11, rsi;

        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        mov rcx, [r11 + 0];
        cmp rax, rcx;
        jne sptd_tear;

        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        mov rcx, [r11 + 8];
        cmp rax, rcx;
        jne sptd_tear;

        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        mov rcx, [r11 + 16];
        cmp rax, rcx;
        jne sptd_tear;

        xor eax, eax;
        ret;

      sptd_tear:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED1B;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // sig_hash_verify_stub(sig_block_ptr: u64, sig_hash_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   UNIMPL placeholder for `blake3_hash32(sig_block_ptr, 3309,
  //   scratch) == *sig_hash_ptr` (ML-DSA-65 signatures are 3309 B --
  //   SIG_ML_DSA_65_SIG_BYTES, src/kernel/core/driver/sig_verify.pdx).
  //   No blake3_hash32 kernel/.pdx primitive exists at this landing
  //   (see file header for the grep evidence and paideia-as
  //   RELEASE-NOTES-v0.20.md's self-hosting audit, which lists "BLAKE3
  //   in .pdx" as a deferred Class-A blocker). Both arguments are
  //   accepted so the frozen ABI needs no call-site edit when the real
  //   primitive lands -- occupies the symbol slot exactly as
  //   pdxfs_lite/verify.pdx's ml_dsa_verify_stub occupies its own.
  //   Unlike that stub (which returns an unconditional PASS because it
  //   sits behind a compile-time-dead flag), this one returns
  //   INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH (0xFFFFED1C) UNCONDITIONALLY -- fail-
  //   closed, because sig_tail_verify calls this in its live path and
  //   an unconditional PASS here would silently accept every signature
  //   block as authentic. Leaf.
  // ============================================================================
  pub let sig_hash_verify_stub : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (sig_block_ptr: u64) (sig_hash_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M4-005 (#1701). UNIMPL stub for blake3-hash-verify -- paideia-as has no blake3_hash32 kernel-callable primitive (confirmed by grep at this landing; RELEASE-NOTES-v0.20.md lists it as a deferred self-hosting Class-A blocker). Fail-closed by design: unconditionally returns INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH (0xFFFFED1C) so no caller can be silently told a signature block's hash matched when no hash was ever computed. Both arguments accepted-but-unused (mov rax,imm; ret) so the ABI the real primitive fills is already frozen -- mirrors pdxfs_lite/verify.pdx's ml_dsa_verify_stub occupying its own symbol slot ahead of the real R32 primitive. Leaf, no callee-save writes, no nested calls.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED1C;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // sig_tail_verify(inode_row_ptr: u64, sig_key_hash_ptr: u64,
  //                 sig_key_material_ptr: u64, sig_block_ptr: u64,
  //                 out_verify_result_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   The umbrella entry point named by #1701's own arity note
  //   (design/filesystem/r52-implementation-plan.md §5.1: "Sig-tail
  //   verify takes (inode_row_ptr, sig_key_hash_ptr,
  //   sig_key_material_ptr, sig_block_ptr, out_verify_result_ptr) = 5.
  //   Fits."). Runs sig_prefix_tear_detect (real); on a tear, stamps
  //   *out_verify_result_ptr = INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR and returns it
  //   WITHOUT touching the hash check (cheap-check-first, matches
  //   volume-fs-substrate.md §2.3's own 2-step ordering: prefix check
  //   before hash check). On a prefix match, runs
  //   sig_hash_verify_stub, stamps *out_verify_result_ptr with its
  //   verdict (currently always INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH -- fail-
  //   closed, see that function's header), and returns it.
  //   sig_key_hash_ptr / sig_key_material_ptr are accepted per the
  //   frozen 5-arg ABI but UNUSED at this landing -- reserved for a
  //   future step-3 ML-DSA-65 call once both blake3_hash32 (paideia-as)
  //   AND ml_dsa_verify (R32, the real lattice verifier -- also absent
  //   today, see file header) exist; that step is unreachable today
  //   regardless, since step 2 always fails closed.
  // ============================================================================
  pub let sig_tail_verify : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (inode_row_ptr: u64) (sig_key_hash_ptr: u64) (sig_key_material_ptr: u64) (sig_block_ptr: u64) (out_verify_result_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R52.M4-005 (#1701). 3-push prologue (rbx=inode_row_ptr, r12=sig_block_ptr, r13=out_verify_result_ptr) lands rsp%16==0 for the 2 nested calls below (entry rsp%16==8 + 24 = 32). sig_key_hash_ptr(rsi)/sig_key_material_ptr(rdx) are read into no register at all -- they are simply not touched after entry, matching pdxfs_sb_verify_sig's own precedent of an ABI-reserved-but-unused argument (see file header: unused pending the step-3 ML-DSA-65 call). Body: (1) rbx=inode_row_ptr(rdi), r12=sig_block_ptr(rcx), r13=out_verify_result_ptr(r8) -- all three saved before any call clobbers the caller-save regs they arrived in. (2) rdi=rbx; rsi=r12; call sig_prefix_tear_detect; cmp rax,0; jne stv_stamp (rax already holds INODE_TAIL_PREFIX_TEAR on that path -- jump straight past the hash check). (3) fallthrough on prefix match: rdi=r12 (sig_block_ptr); rsi=rbx; add rsi,96 (&inode_row.sig_hash); call sig_hash_verify_stub (rax now holds its verdict, currently always INODE_TAIL_HASH_MISMATCH). stv_stamp: mov [r13+0],rax (stamps whichever verdict rax carries). 3-pop epilogue + ret. No test instruction; no mov_b (whole-qword stamp). Reserved labels: stv_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;
        mov r12, rcx;
        mov r13, r8;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        call sig_prefix_tear_detect;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne stv_stamp;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, rbx;
        add rsi, 96;
        call sig_hash_verify_stub;

      stv_stamp:
        mov [r13 + 0], rax;

        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
